# Migrating a flat Pulumi TS program to a `ComponentResource` — zero replacements

## 0. Why this is risky at all

Pulumi identity is the **URN**, and the URN embeds the *parent type chain*:

```
before:  urn:pulumi:prod::acme-app::aws:s3/bucket:BucketV2::assets
after:   urn:pulumi:prod::acme-app::acme:app:AppStack$aws:s3/bucket:BucketV2::assets
                                     ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ new parent segment
```

To the engine that is a *different resource*. Without help you get `create` + `delete` for every resource in the program — i.e. a new RDS instance, a new bucket (data loss), a new EKS cluster. `aliases` tells the engine "this new URN is the old URN", so the diff collapses to **no changes**.

Two things that are *not* the fix and are worth naming:

* `pulumi state rename` / `pulumi state move` — imperative, unreviewable, no PR artifact, and doesn't handle 20 resources atomically.
* `protect: true` — stops the delete, doesn't stop the create; you end up with duplicates.

---

## 1. Before: the flat program

`index.ts` (abridged — this is the state we're migrating *from*):

```ts
import * as aws from "@pulumi/aws";
import * as eks from "@pulumi/eks";
import * as pulumi from "@pulumi/pulumi";

const cfg = new pulumi.Config();
const env = pulumi.getStack();                    // "dev" | "prod"
const nodeCount = cfg.requireNumber("nodeCount");
const nodeType  = cfg.require("nodeInstanceType");
const dbClass   = cfg.require("dbInstanceClass");
const isProd    = env === "prod";

const bucket = new aws.s3.BucketV2("assets", { /* ... */ });
const bucketVersioning = new aws.s3.BucketVersioningV2("assets-versioning", { /* ... */ });
const bucketSse = new aws.s3.BucketServerSideEncryptionConfigurationV2("assets-sse", { /* ... */ });
const bucketPab = new aws.s3.BucketPublicAccessBlock("assets-pab", { /* ... */ });
const db = new aws.rds.Instance("appdb", { /* ... */ });
const cluster = new eks.Cluster("app", { /* ... */ });
const svcUser = new aws.iam.User("svc", { /* ... */ });
// ...cdn, acm, role, policy
```

Everything is parented to the implicit root stack resource.

---

## 2. After: the component

### Layout

```
.
├── Pulumi.yaml
├── Pulumi.dev.yaml
├── Pulumi.prod.yaml
├── index.ts                  # thin: read config, instantiate component
└── components/
    ├── appStack.ts           # acme:app:AppStack
    ├── storage.ts            # acme:app:Storage
    ├── database.ts           # acme:app:Database
    ├── cluster.ts            # acme:app:Cluster
    ├── identity.ts           # acme:app:Identity
    └── edge.ts               # acme:app:Edge
```

### The alias rule (the whole trick)

> Every resource whose **parent changed** needs
> `aliases: [{ parent: pulumi.rootStackResource }]`.
>
> Aliases on a `ComponentResource` **propagate to its children automatically**, so you only need the explicit alias at the boundary where the old parent was the root.

Because I introduced *nested* components (`AppStack` → `Storage` → bucket), the bucket's parent chain changed by **two** segments. Rather than hand-write a compound alias, I put the root alias on the **leaf resources** — that is unambiguous and independent of how deeply I nest later.

### `components/storage.ts`

```ts
import * as aws from "@pulumi/aws";
import * as pulumi from "@pulumi/pulumi";

export interface StorageArgs {
  env: string;
  replicaRegion?: string;              // prod only
  replicationRole?: aws.iam.Role;
}

export class Storage extends pulumi.ComponentResource {
  public readonly bucket: aws.s3.BucketV2;
  public readonly bucketArn: pulumi.Output<string>;

  constructor(name: string, args: StorageArgs, opts?: pulumi.ComponentResourceOptions) {
    super("acme:app:Storage", name, {}, opts);
    const parented = { parent: this };

    // NOTE: logical names ("assets", "assets-versioning", ...) are UNCHANGED
    // from the flat program. Only the parent changed -> only a parent alias.
    this.bucket = new aws.s3.BucketV2("assets", {
      tags: { Environment: args.env, ManagedBy: "pulumi" },
    }, {
      ...parented,
      aliases: [{ parent: pulumi.rootStackResource }],
      // belt-and-braces during the migration window; removed after cutover
      protect: args.env === "prod",
    });

    new aws.s3.BucketVersioningV2("assets-versioning", {
      bucket: this.bucket.id,
      versioningConfiguration: { status: "Enabled" },
    }, { ...parented, aliases: [{ parent: pulumi.rootStackResource }] });

    new aws.s3.BucketServerSideEncryptionConfigurationV2("assets-sse", {
      bucket: this.bucket.id,
      rules: [{
        applyServerSideEncryptionByDefault: { sseAlgorithm: "AES256" },
        bucketKeyEnabled: true,
      }],
    }, { ...parented, aliases: [{ parent: pulumi.rootStackResource }] });

    new aws.s3.BucketPublicAccessBlock("assets-pab", {
      bucket: this.bucket.id,
      blockPublicAcls: true, blockPublicPolicy: true,
      ignorePublicAcls: true, restrictPublicBuckets: true,
    }, { ...parented, aliases: [{ parent: pulumi.rootStackResource }] });

    if (args.replicaRegion && args.replicationRole) {
      // prod-only CRR; NEW resource, no alias (it never existed before under
      // another URN — if it did exist flat, it would need one too).
      new aws.s3.BucketReplicationConfig("assets-replication", {
        bucket: this.bucket.id,
        role: args.replicationRole.arn,
        rules: [{
          id: "replicate-all",
          status: "Enabled",
          filter: {},
          deleteMarkerReplication: { status: "Enabled" },
          destination: { bucket: pulumi.interpolate`arn:aws:s3:::assets-replica-${args.env}` },
        }],
      }, { ...parented, aliases: [{ parent: pulumi.rootStackResource }] });
    }

    this.bucketArn = this.bucket.arn;
    this.registerOutputs({ bucketArn: this.bucketArn });
  }
}
```

### `components/database.ts`

```ts
export class Database extends pulumi.ComponentResource {
  public readonly instance: aws.rds.Instance;
  public readonly connectionString: pulumi.Output<string>;

  constructor(name: string, args: DatabaseArgs, opts?: pulumi.ComponentResourceOptions) {
    super("acme:app:Database", name, {}, opts);
    const parented = { parent: this };
    const isProd = args.env === "prod";

    const subnetGroup = new aws.rds.SubnetGroup("appdb-subnets", {
      subnetIds: args.privateSubnetIds,
    }, { ...parented, aliases: [{ parent: pulumi.rootStackResource }] });

    this.instance = new aws.rds.Instance("appdb", {
      engine: "postgres",
      engineVersion: "16.3",
      instanceClass: args.instanceClass,          // db.t3.micro | db.t3.medium
      allocatedStorage: isProd ? 100 : 20,
      multiAz: isProd,
      storageEncrypted: true,                     // AC: encrypted (prod spec; on for both)
      kmsKeyId: isProd ? args.kmsKeyArn : undefined,
      dbSubnetGroupName: subnetGroup.name,
      vpcSecurityGroupIds: [args.securityGroupId],
      username: args.username,
      password: args.password,                    // pulumi.Output<string>, secret
      // --- acceptance criteria ---
      deletionProtection: true,
      backupRetentionPeriod: isProd ? 30 : 7,     // >= 7
      publiclyAccessible: false,
      // ---------------------------
      skipFinalSnapshot: false,
      finalSnapshotIdentifier: `appdb-${args.env}-final`,
      applyImmediately: false,
    }, {
      ...parented,
      aliases: [{ parent: pulumi.rootStackResource }],
      protect: true,
      // instanceClass/multiAz changes are in-place modifies, but guard anyway:
      ignoreChanges: [],
    });

    this.connectionString = pulumi.secret(
      pulumi.interpolate`postgresql://${args.username}:${args.password}@${this.instance.endpoint}/${args.dbName}`
    );

    this.registerOutputs({ endpoint: this.instance.endpoint });
  }
}
```

### `components/appStack.ts` (the composition root)

```ts
export interface AppStackArgs {
  env: "dev" | "prod";
  nodeCount: number;
  nodeInstanceType: string;
  dbInstanceClass: string;
  dbUsername: pulumi.Input<string>;
  dbPassword: pulumi.Output<string>;     // from config.requireSecret
  domainName?: string;
  oidcProviderArn?: pulumi.Input<string>;
  replicaRegion?: string;
}

export class AppStack extends pulumi.ComponentResource {
  public readonly kubeconfig: pulumi.Output<string>;
  public readonly bucketName: pulumi.Output<string>;
  public readonly dbConnectionString: pulumi.Output<string>;
  public readonly url: pulumi.Output<string>;

  constructor(name: string, args: AppStackArgs, opts?: pulumi.ComponentResourceOptions) {
    super("acme:app:AppStack", name, {}, opts);
    const child = { parent: this };
    const isProd = args.env === "prod";

    const net      = new Network("net", { env: args.env }, child);
    const identity = new Identity("identity", {
      env: args.env, oidcProviderArn: args.oidcProviderArn,
    }, child);
    const storage  = new Storage("storage", {
      env: args.env,
      replicaRegion: isProd ? args.replicaRegion : undefined,
      replicationRole: isProd ? identity.replicationRole : undefined,
    }, child);
    const database = new Database("database", {
      env: args.env,
      instanceClass: args.dbInstanceClass,
      privateSubnetIds: net.privateSubnetIds,
      securityGroupId: net.dbSecurityGroupId,
      username: args.dbUsername,
      password: args.dbPassword,
      dbName: "app",
      kmsKeyArn: identity.kmsKeyArn,
    }, child);
    const cluster  = new Cluster("cluster", {
      env: args.env,
      nodeCount: args.nodeCount,
      instanceType: args.nodeInstanceType,
      vpcId: net.vpcId,
      subnetIds: net.privateSubnetIds,
    }, child);
    const edge     = new Edge("edge", {
      env: args.env,
      domainName: args.domainName,
      originBucket: storage.bucket,
      albDnsName: cluster.albDnsName,
    }, child);

    this.kubeconfig         = cluster.kubeconfig;
    this.bucketName         = storage.bucket.bucket;
    this.dbConnectionString = database.connectionString;
    this.url                = edge.url;

    this.registerOutputs({
      kubeconfig: this.kubeconfig,
      bucketName: this.bucketName,
      url: this.url,
    });
  }
}
```

### `index.ts`

```ts
import * as pulumi from "@pulumi/pulumi";
import { AppStack } from "./components/appStack";

const cfg = new pulumi.Config();
const env = pulumi.getStack() as "dev" | "prod";

const app = new AppStack("app", {
  env,
  nodeCount:        cfg.requireNumber("nodeCount"),
  nodeInstanceType: cfg.require("nodeInstanceType"),
  dbInstanceClass:  cfg.require("dbInstanceClass"),
  dbUsername:       cfg.require("dbUsername"),
  dbPassword:       cfg.requireSecret("dbPassword"),   // encrypted at rest
  domainName:       cfg.get("domainName"),
  oidcProviderArn:  cfg.get("oidcProviderArn"),
  replicaRegion:    cfg.get("replicaRegion"),
});

export const kubeconfig = pulumi.secret(app.kubeconfig);
export const bucketName = app.bucketName;
export const dbConnectionString = app.dbConnectionString;   // already secret
export const url = app.url;
```

### Stack configs

`Pulumi.dev.yaml`
```yaml
config:
  aws:region: us-east-1
  acme-app:nodeCount: "2"
  acme-app:nodeInstanceType: t3.medium
  acme-app:dbInstanceClass: db.t3.micro
  acme-app:dbUsername: appuser
  acme-app:dbPassword:
    secure: AAABAJ...            # pulumi config set --secret
```

`Pulumi.prod.yaml`
```yaml
config:
  aws:region: us-east-1
  acme-app:nodeCount: "4"
  acme-app:nodeInstanceType: t3.large
  acme-app:dbInstanceClass: db.t3.medium
  acme-app:replicaRegion: us-west-2
  acme-app:domainName: app.example.com
  acme-app:oidcProviderArn: arn:aws:iam::123456789012:oidc-provider/...
  acme-app:dbPassword:
    secure: AAABAP...
```

Dev and prod are separate stacks → separate state files → environment isolation is structural, not conventional.

---

## 3. If you *also* renamed resources

Common temptation during a refactor: `"assets"` → `"app-assets"`. That changes the URN's name segment too. Then the alias needs both:

```ts
new aws.s3.BucketV2("app-assets", { /* ... */ }, {
  parent: this,
  aliases: [{ name: "assets", parent: pulumi.rootStackResource }],
});
```

**Recommendation: don't do both in one PR.** Migrate parents first with names untouched (one reviewable diff, `preview` must show `0 changes`), then rename in a follow-up. Two small verifiable steps beat one big one.

If you'd rather not hand-write per-resource aliases, the alternative is a single alias on the component plus name-preserving children — but that only works when the component's *own* URN is the one that changed, which isn't our case (the component is brand new).

**Escape hatch for gnarly cases:** aliases accept a literal URN string, which sidesteps all inference:

```ts
aliases: [{ urn: "urn:pulumi:prod::acme-app::aws:s3/bucket:BucketV2::assets" }]
```

Get the exact strings from `pulumi stack export | jq -r '.deployment.resources[].urn'`.

---

## 4. Verification procedure

This is the part that makes the migration trustworthy.

```bash
# 0. Back up state for both stacks. Non-negotiable.
for s in dev prod; do
  pulumi stack select $s
  pulumi stack export --file "backup-$s-$(date +%Y%m%dT%H%M%S).json"
done

# 1. Snapshot the pre-migration URN set
pulumi stack select dev
pulumi stack export | jq -r '.deployment.resources[].urn' | sort > /tmp/urns-before.txt

# 2. Preview on DEV first. The bar is literally zero changes.
pulumi preview --diff --stack dev
```

Expected output:

```
Previewing update (dev):
     Type                 Name          Plan
     pulumi:pulumi:Stack  acme-app-dev
 +   └─ acme:app:AppStack app           create      # component: state-only, no cloud call
 +      ├─ acme:app:Storage  storage    create
 +      ├─ acme:app:Database database   create
 ...
Resources:
    + 8 to create        <-- ONLY ComponentResources
```

The gate: **every `+` line must be an `acme:app:*` type.** If a single `aws:*` or `eks:*` appears in the create or replace list, an alias is missing or wrong — stop and fix.

Also assert nothing is being *deleted*:

```bash
pulumi preview --json --stack dev \
  | jq -e '[.steps[] | select(.op=="delete" or .op=="replace" or .op=="create")
            | select(.urn | test("::acme:app:") | not)] | length == 0' \
  && echo "SAFE" || echo "UNSAFE — missing alias"
```

Then:

```bash
pulumi up --stack dev
pulumi stack export | jq -r '.deployment.resources[].urn' \
  | grep -v '::acme:app:' | sed 's/acme:app:[A-Za-z]*\$//g' | sort > /tmp/urns-after.txt
diff /tmp/urns-before.txt /tmp/urns-after.txt   # should be empty modulo the parent segment
```

Physical-identity check (the thing that actually matters):

```bash
aws rds describe-db-instances --db-instance-identifier <id> \
  --query 'DBInstances[0].DbiResourceId'   # must match pre-migration value
aws s3api get-bucket-versioning --bucket <name>
```

Only then repeat for `prod`, ideally with `pulumi up --diff` and a human reading the plan.

---

## 5. Cleanup — and the part people forget

Once `pulumi up` succeeds, state records the *new* URNs. The aliases are now inert.

**Do not delete them immediately.** Leave them for at least one release cycle:

* Any stack that hasn't been upgraded yet (a stale branch, a preview env, a DR stack in another account) still holds the old URNs. Removing aliases before *every* stack has been updated re-arms the replacement.
* Track it explicitly: a checklist of stacks, ticked off as each one completes a successful `up`.

Then remove in a dedicated PR whose preview must, again, show zero changes.

```ts
// TODO(#1423): remove after dev+prod+dr-usw2 have all completed the component
// migration. Verified: dev 2024-xx-xx, prod 2024-xx-xx, dr-usw2 PENDING.
aliases: [{ parent: pulumi.rootStackResource }],
```

Two other residues to clear:

* The `protect: true` flags added as a migration seatbelt — keep them on RDS/S3 in prod as policy, drop the ones you added purely for the migration.
* Any `--target` scoping you used to rehearse the migration piecemeal.

---

## 6. Honest limitations

* **Aliases only fix identity, not drift.** If the refactor also changed a property (say you "tidied" `backupRetentionPeriod` from 7 to 30), the preview shows an update — correct, but make sure it's intentional and not smuggled in alongside the alias change. Keep the migration PR property-identical.
* **Provider-level replacements are unaffected.** If you simultaneously bump the AWS provider major version, replace-triggering property changes can still fire. Migrate parents on a pinned provider.
* **`pulumi.rootStackResource` is only meaningful inside a running program.** It resolves to the stack resource for the *current* stack; it's not a cross-stack reference.
* **`registerOutputs` matters.** Omitting it doesn't break the migration but makes component outputs invisible to `pulumi stack output` and to `getResource` lookups downstream.

---

## Where this stack lands on the scenario

Pulumi's real advantage here is that this is *ordinary program refactoring* — extract a class, pass args, unit-test the component with the mock monitor — while `aliases` provides a declarative, code-reviewed, version-controlled statement of identity continuity. The migration diff is a PR, the safety check is `preview`, and the rollback is `pulumi stack import` of the backup.

The cost is that identity is implicit in the URN and therefore in the *shape of your code*. Restructuring code restructures identity, and you must remember to say so. Stacks that key on explicit user-assigned IDs don't have this failure mode; stacks that key on file paths have a worse version of it. Pulumi's `aliases` is a good answer to a problem Pulumi's design creates.
